3. Not fixed — compiler segfaults (SIGSEGV in `lower_func_body`, while lowering `string.hy`'s `slice()`) whenever a user function's return value feeds an `if/else` and `std.io`/`std.string` is in scope. Possibly the same underlying defect as #2 (something about branch-lowering state after a call).
4. No dead-code elimination for included stdlib modules — every function in `std.os.exec` gets lowered/linked even if you only call one. Bugs in unused stdlib functions can crash builds that never touch them (as above).
5. `\n`/`\t` and other escapes in string literals are never processed (`lower.c` just strips quotes) — `"\n"` is literally backslash-n.

**Compiler — QoL**

6. `exec()`/`spawn()` use raw `execve`, which doesn't search `$PATH` (unlike `execvp`). Worth a `spawn_path()`/`execvp`-style helper so `spawn("echo", ...)` works the way people expect from a shell.
7. Add `dup2`-based stdout/stderr/stdin redirection to `exec()`/`spawn()` (Go's `cmd.Stdout = ...`), covered earlier — `NR_DUP2`/`NR_PIPE` already exist in `platform/linux_x86_64.hy`, just need the wrapper functions.

**LSP**

8. Include-path completion inserts the full `std.xxx` label with no `textEdit`/replace range (`lsp_main.c` completion response only sets `"label"`) — editors default to replacing back to the last `.`, so tab-completing after typing `std.` yields `std.std.os.exec`. Fix: emit a `textEdit` with a range covering the whole partial dotted path.
9. LSP's own grammar (`lsp/src/parser_lsp.y`) has no `else if` — only `IF (...) {} ELSE {}`. The real compiler grammar (`compiler/parser.y`) has the recursive `ELSE if_stmt` rule and handles it fine. So valid `else if` code gets a false "syntax error, unexpected IF, expecting LBRACE" from the LSP while actually compiling and running correctly.
10. Root cause of #9: there are three independent grammar copies (`compiler/parser.y`, `lsp/src/parser_lsp.y`, and a tree-sitter grammar) that can silently drift. Worth unifying, or at minimum a test that diffs their accepted-syntax sets.

